# arvo. Architecture Design

> Numeric primitives whose behaviour is the consumer's to select, through
> strategy markers at type level. `#![no_std]`, no alloc. Zero dependency
> on platform. Designed so every downstream consumer shares one
> numeric layer.

## Principles

The primitives are parameterised by strategy. A numeral's behaviour is to be
selected at type level by a strategy, without runtime dispatch. `Hot`, `Warm`, `Cold` and
`Precise` ship as presets and the set is open. No value in the tree takes a
strategy yet, since `Num` is parameterised by a declared signature and its
aliases by an adaptation.

Every crate is `no_std` with no allocation, and every size is const, so
consumers pre-allocate and arvo never calls into an allocator.

The crates are written against trait contracts and are generic over trait
bounds, so downstream code picks its own concrete instantiations.

Monomorphisation is the dispatch. There is no `dyn` and no `TypeId`, and the
monomorphised function is the type identity.

Everything answerable before the program runs is answered there, in plain
`const fn`. The operations wanted in const context are inherent `const fn`s on
their types. The contracts a numeral satisfies are const traits in
`arvo-contracts`, which with `arvo-numeral`, implementing them, takes
`const_trait_impl` as it builds by default. `arvo-bits` takes it too, for the const
traits of its bit tier and its packed word. No other crate enables an unstable
feature by default, and one cargo feature of `arvo-numeral`, off by default,
enables the same gate for its const impl of another crate's trait. A consumer
bounding on a contract, naming a value or calling an inherent method or the tier
from an ordinary function needs no feature of the toolchain for any of it, per
`obligation::the_unstable_machinery_does_not_reach_a_consumer`; only code
evaluating a const trait through a `[const]` bound in a `const fn` of its own, or
writing a `const impl` of one, enables the gate itself, where it asks for that.

## Crate topology

Eight crates. Four of them chain by dependency, and the dependency direction is
what enforces the design rather than a rule asking for it: `arvo-format` cannot
name a carrier because it does not depend on the crate that has one, which is
how "every operation is a function of the declared width and never of the
machine carrier" stays true under later edits. The fifth, `arvo-bits`, sits
above `arvo-format` and beside `arvo-placement`, off the chain, on `arvo-format`, whose truth value
and count of bits its bit operations answer in, and on notko and `notko-hlist`, whose list a packed word's layout is. The sixth, `arvo-digest`, sits on `arvo-bits`, whose door its bytes come
through and whose container holds its result, and on `arvo-numeral`, whose
wrapping product FNV-1a multiplies by. The seventh, `arvo-contracts`, sits directly above
`arvo-format` and below `arvo-numeral`, depending on `arvo-format` alone. The
bridge-home rule would put its contracts in `arvo-format`, the lowest crate
reaching their types; they sit in a crate of their own because a contract a
value implements is not the subject of the crate that declares the signature,
following `proposal::a_value_contract_is_not_the_format_crates_subject`. The
eighth, `arvo-capacity`, sits on `arvo-numeral`, whose platform-sized integer
its count is read in.

| Crate | What it ships | Depends on |
|---|---|---|
| `arvo-format` | The declared signature. The representable set as one affine predicate over a phase, a quantum law and a slot range, of which integers, fixed point, scaled integers and floats are points. The six rounding modes, the overflow policies, the adaptation as the pair of those two, and the map that applies one. Addition, negation, subtraction, multiplication and division, and converting a member onto another declared grid, each the exact result adapted once, with the verdict on where the adapted sum is associative. Introduces the numeric category, so `Width` and `Bool` are defined here. | `notko` |
| `arvo-contracts` | The contracts a numeral satisfies, as const traits: sum, difference and product with their `fits` predicates, the order, the additive and multiplicative identity, sign, magnitude, the member at a constant slot, the monotone sum selected by the overflow policy, and the reciprocal and square root named with no instance. A consumer bounding on one depends on this crate alone. | `arvo-format` |
| `arvo-placement` | The placement derivation, keyed on occupancy. One output at sole occupancy, the carrier, given also as a type per slot family; three at shared, the carrier and the access width and the stride. No declared operation reads any of them. | `arvo-format` |
| `arvo-strategy` | The binding from a name to a placement objective and an adaptation selection. Four presets ship as instances of an open concept rather than as members of an enumeration. | `arvo-format`, `arvo-placement` |
| `arvo-numeral` | The value. `Num<S>` holds one member of a declared signature alone in the carrier `arvo-placement` derives at sole occupancy, and its sum, computed in that carrier, gives the slot the format's own sum gives. Comparison, the doors in and out, the additive identity where the format has one, and the aliases `Uint`, `Int`, `USize` and `ISize`. A debug check the consumer chooses, aborting a debug build by default when a result leaves the range. | `arvo-format`, `arvo-placement`, `arvo-contracts` |
| `arvo-bits` | An exact-width bit container, `Bits<const N: u32>` for `N` from one to sixty-four, and `Bytes`, the host's byte slice walked one `Bits<8>` at a time. Masked construction and masked cast between declared widths, a 128-bit host value split into two halves, and the bit tier: one bit read and written, `and`, `or`, `xor`, `not` and the emptiness test at one width, shifts and rotations within it, each a `const fn` and each again behind the traits `BitWidth`, `BitAccess`, `BitLogic` and `BitSequence`, whose refusal of an index past the width a container declares the tier owns, with the proof `Within`, for every container. The packed word, `Word<L>`, a word of named fields of declared widths listed from the low bit up, each read and written in a constant, a one-bit field read as `Bool`, and a layout whose widths do not sum to the word's refused at build. No arithmetic, no sign. Introduces the numeric category a second time, so `Bits` is defined here rather than reaching into `arvo-format` for it. | `arvo-format`, `notko`, `notko-hlist` |
| `arvo-digest` | A digest of explicit bytes, `Digest<const BITS: u32>` at 32, 64 and 128 bits. FNV-1a at 32 and 64 bits and XXH3 at 64 and 128 by name, each with a `const fn` form equal to its runtime one; a switched digest joins once its crossover is measured. Narrows into `Bits<N>` at the width a consumer names. Not cryptographic. | `arvo-format`, `arvo-numeral`, `arvo-bits`, `notko`, `xxhash-rust` |
| `arvo-capacity` | A count fixed when the consumer builds, carried as a type. `Capacity` projects an array of exactly its count, read and written as a slice, and `Dim<N>` is the capacity of `N` elements; capacities bundle under one parameter and nest with no product computed, and `count` reads one in a constant as a `USize` under the adaptation the consumer names. Sealed to `Dim<N>` while that question is open. | `arvo-format`, `arvo-numeral` |

The set is open at both ends it can be. A new numeral joins by implementing
`Format`, a new overflow policy by implementing `Overflow`, and a new strategy by
implementing `Strategy`, none of which edits anything that already exists. The
rounding vocabulary is the one closed enumeration, fixed at six names, so a
const predicate over it is exhaustive and a seventh mode is a different design
rather than a new row.

## Contracts and their instances

Every crate here is a small set of contracts plus the instances that ship with
them. `Format`, `Ambient`, `Quantum`, `Slots`, `Rounding`, `Overflow`,
`Adaptation`, `DeclaredSignature` and `Operation` are contracts; `Integer`,
`UFixed`, `Biased`, `Floating`, the six modes, the three policies and the
operations `Add`, `Neg`, `Sub`, `Mul`, `Div` and `Convert` are instances of
them. In `arvo-numeral`, `Sole`, `DebugCheck` and `NamesAdaptation` are contracts,
and `Abort` and `Quiet` are instances. `arvo-contracts` declares the contracts
a numeral satisfies, `Sum`, `Difference`, `Product`, `Order`, `Identity`, `Sign`,
`Magnitude`, `Constant`, `MonotoneSum`, `MonotoneCompletion`, `Reciprocal` and
`SquareRoot`, with `Additive` and `Multiplicative` its instances of an identity's
kind; `Num` is the instance of every numeric contract, and a consumer's own type
can be one on the same terms.

In `arvo-bits`, `BitWidth`, `BitAccessAt`, `BitAccess`, `BitLogic`, `BitSequenceBy` and
`BitSequence` are contracts and `Bits<const N: u32>` is their instance, with `Within` the proof the
tier makes for an index below the width a container declares. The container itself has
no contract behind it, because an exact-width value at a declared width has one
shape, and a second implementation would not be a new instance of a concept, it
would be a second definition of the same fact. What a second container shares
with it is the bit operations, so those are the contracts, and an array of
`Bits<64>` holding a wider set implements them in the crate that holds it.
The packed word adds a contract the container and every field value share, `Lane`,
and two a consumer implements, `Layout` and `Field`; `Word<L>` is the word of
a layout, and `Fields` and `Below<P>` are the sealed folds over its list.

The reason the split falls this way is what a consumer has to depend on to say
one thing. Stating what values exist needs `arvo-format` and nothing else.
Stating where they sit needs `arvo-placement` on top of it. Choosing between
placements needs `arvo-strategy` on top of both. Writing code generic over a numeral needs `arvo-contracts` and nothing
above `arvo-format`, so it builds against the shape before or without any
instance. Holding a value and computing
with it needs `arvo-numeral`, which builds on the first two and not on the
strategy binding. Saying the same thing in an
industrial convention's own words is still stating what values exist, so it needs
`arvo-format` and nothing else, and the conventions ship there beside the
parameterisation points. A consumer that only declares formats never compiles a
carrier ladder. Naming a value at an exact width needs `arvo-bits`, and under
it `arvo-format` for the truth value and the count of bits the bit operations
answer in, and nothing of the placement, the strategy binding or the value.

## Dependency direction

Strictly upward within the chain, and it carries a design constraint
rather than a convention. `arvo-format` has no workspace dependency, so nothing
in it can mention a carrier; `arvo-placement` depends on it and derives one;
`arvo-strategy` depends on both and binds a name to a choice between them.
`arvo-contracts` depends on `arvo-format` alone and declares the contracts a
numeral satisfies, so code generic over a numeral compiles no carrier.
`arvo-numeral` depends on the first two and on `arvo-contracts`, holds a value
in the carrier the placement derives, reading the format for everything it
computes, and implements every contract for it.
`arvo-bits` depends on `arvo-format` for `Bool` and `Width`, on notko for
`Maybe` and on `notko-hlist` for the list a layout's fields are, and on nothing that derives or holds a carrier: the value it holds and the format that
would describe a numeral's representable set are answers to different
questions, so it takes the format's vocabulary and none of its formats. `arvo-digest`
depends on `arvo-bits` for the bytes it reads, the container its result is held
in and the width a consumer narrows it to, on `arvo-numeral` for the
wrapping product and on `arvo-format` for the adaptation that product is taken
under, so it writes neither the walk's door nor its arithmetic. There is no facade today, and a
crate that re-exported the eight would sit below all of them rather than above.
`arvo-capacity` depends on `arvo-numeral` for the integer a count is read in and
on `arvo-format` for the adaptation it is read under, and on nothing that holds
an array, since the array it projects is the language's own.

## Consumers

arvo is a library rather than a program, so how it gets used is not fully known
here. What it is built for is the algorithm crates every downstream repository
uses, and the contracts for things that compose into units bigger than numerals
alone. The numeral base has to work first, which is what the crates above are.

The consumers named in earlier versions of this document, hilavitkutin and
vehje, used crates that no longer exist. Those crates were removed on
2026-08-08 and the surfaces they offered have not been rebuilt on the current
base. A downstream repository pinning this branch gets the crates above and
nothing else, and that is the honest statement of what is available rather than
of what is planned. `arvo-numeral` is the first value type on the current base:
integers and the other constant-quantum points, with their sum, difference,
negation, product, quotient, conversion and comparison, and nothing yet of a
remainder. `arvo-bits` is new source written from the current canon,
not a restoration of anything removed, and it answers one piece of what those
removed crates used to answer, an exact-width container, rather than the rest
of it. Hashing is `arvo-digest`'s, new source
rather than restored from the removed hash crate. A count fixed at build that sizes
arrays is `arvo-capacity`'s, new source carrying a count as a type rather than restoring
the removed tensor crate, and string identity is not shipped here.

